.TH "UBERTOOTH\-SPECAN\-UI" "1" "October 2026" "Project Ubertooth" "User Commands"
.SH "NAME"
\fBubertooth\-specan\-ui\fR \- graphical 2\.4 GHz spectrum analyzer for Ubertooth
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.nf
ubertooth\-specan\-ui [\-U <index|serial>] [\-l <freq>] [\-u <freq>]
[\-\-wifi[=<channel(s)>]] [\-\-padding <MHz>]
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
ubertooth\-specan\-ui(1) shows the 2\.4 GHz band as a live spectrum plot in a window\. It runs ubertooth\-specan(1) in the background and draws what it reports, so it needs an attached Ubertooth running the \fBbluetooth_rxtx\fR firmware, Python 3\.8 or newer, and the Python packages \fBnumpy\fR, \fBqtpy\fR and a Qt binding (\fBPySide6\fR or \fBPyQt5\fR)\.
.P
To show the whole band on the only attached Ubertooth, run:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-specan\-ui
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
To pick one of several Ubertooths, run:
.IP "" 4
.nf
ubertooth\-specan\-ui \-U 0
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-U <index|serial>\fR
select an Ubertooth by enumeration index or serial number; see ubertooth(7), SELECTING A DEVICE
.TP
\fB\-l <2402\-2480>\fR
lower frequency in MHz (default 2402)
.TP
\fB\-u <2402\-2480>\fR
upper frequency in MHz (default 2480); must not be lower than \fB\-l\fR\. The plot is drawn 2 MHz wider than the scanned range on each side, within 2400\-2483 MHz, so the edge channels can be marked
.TP
\fB\-\-wifi[=<channel(s)>]\fR
show the band around one Wi\-Fi channel (for example \fB\-\-wifi=6\fR) or a range of channels (\fB\-\-wifi=1\-11\fR); \fB\-\-wifi\fR alone shows channel 1\. Channel 14 is centred on 2484 MHz, above the 2480 MHz the board can tune, so \fB\-\-wifi=14\fR shows the part of that channel below 2480 MHz \-\- and with a \fB\-\-padding\fR under 4 it is refused, because then the whole band asked for is above the range
.TP
\fB\-\-padding <MHz>\fR
extra band shown on each side with \fB\-\-wifi\fR (default 10), limited to 2402\-2480 MHz
.SH "DISPLAY"
.IP "\(bu" 4
White trace: the current sweep\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Green trace: the highest level seen recently (max hold)\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Red dotted lines: the strongest peak, labelled with its frequency and level\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Cyan dotted line, labelled "Avg: N dBm": the average of the green trace across the band, smoothed so it moves slowly\.
.IP "\(bu" 4
Yellow and magenta lines: points marked with the left and right mouse buttons, with the frequency differences to the peak and to each other\.
.IP "" 0
.SH "KEYS AND MOUSE"
Press \fBh\fR in the window to show this list on screen\. Keys work in upper or lower case\. There are two mouse markers, one per button: clicking again moves that marker\. On a Mac trackpad, right click is a two\-finger click or Control\-click\.
.TP
\fBleft click\fR
set marker 1 (yellow) at the pointer's frequency and level
.TP
\fBright click\fR
set marker 2 (magenta); the frequency differences to marker 1 and to the peak are shown
.TP
\fBmiddle click\fR or \fBm\fR
show or hide the peak and average markers, and clear the mouse markers
.TP
\fBc\fR
clear the graph
.TP
\fBh\fR
show or hide the key help
.TP
\fBq\fR
quit
.SH "DIAGNOSTICS"
A range \fBubertooth\-specan\-ui\fR will not take is refused before the window opens, with one line starting \fBerror:\fR on standard error and exit status 1: \fBerror: invalid channel range: <value>\fR, \fBerror: channel <n> is not a valid wifi channel\fR, \fBerror: frequency of <n> MHz is below minimum frequency of <min>\fR, \fBerror: frequency of <n> MHz is above maximum frequency of <max>\fR and \fBerror: lower frequency of <n> MHz is above upper frequency of <n> MHz\fR\. They used to be \fBERROR: \|\.\|\.\|\.\fR on standard output\.
.P
A failure to start \fBubertooth\-specan\fR is reported the same way, as \fBerror: could not open an Ubertooth device\fR followed by a \fBnote:\fR naming the command, and whatever \fBubertooth\-specan\fR itself said on standard error\.
.P
The key help \fBh\fR shows goes to standard output, as what the user asked for\. The notes about an unknown or unsupported key are \fBnote:\fR lines on standard error\. \fB\-\-help\fR comes from the argument parser: standard output, exit 0\.
